  "answer_text": "A **Custodial IRA** (Individual Retirement Account) is a tax-advantaged retirement savings account that an adult (usually a parent or grandparent) opens and manages on behalf of a minor child. \n\nBecause minors cannot legally open brokerage accounts or enter into binding financial contracts, the adult acts as the **custodian**. The custodian manages the investments and makes decisions for the account until the child reaches the age of majority (usually 18 or 21, depending on state law), at which point full control of the account is officially transferred to the child.\n\n---\n\n### Key Rules of a Custodial IRA\n\n#### 1. The Earned Income Rule\nThis is the most critical rule for opening a Custodial IRA: **the child must have earned income**. \n* **What counts:** Wages from a W-2 part-time job (like working at a grocery store or lifeguarding) or self-employment income from informal jobs (like babysitting, mowing lawns, or dog walking).\n* **What does NOT count:** Allowances, investment returns, or cash gifts.\n* *Note:* The contributed money does not have to physically come from the child\u2019s paycheck. A parent or grandparent can contribute their own cash to the account as a \"match\" or gift, as long as the total contributed does not exceed what the child actually earned that year.\n\n#### 2. Contribution Limits\nThe maximum amount that can be contributed to a Custodial IRA each year is the lesser of:\n* **The child\u2019s total earned income** for that year.\n* **The annual IRS limit** for IRAs. \n  * **2025 Limit:** $7,000\n  * **2026 Limit:** $7,500\n\n*Example:* If a teenager earns $3,000 mowing lawns in 2026, the maximum that can be contributed to their Custodial IRA is $3,000. If they work a summer job and earn $10,000, the maximum contribution is capped at the IRS limit of $7,500.\n\n#### 3. Traditional vs. Roth Custodial IRAs\nJust like standard adult IRAs, you can choose between two main types, though one is almost always better for kids:\n* **Custodial Roth IRA (Most Popular):** Contributions are made with after-tax dollars. Because children generally earn very little, they are usually in the lowest tax bracket and pay little to no income tax anyway. The money in the account grows tax-free, and all qualified withdrawals in retirement are 100% tax-free. \n* **Custodial Traditional IRA:** Contributions may be tax-deductible today, but withdrawals in retirement are taxed as ordinary income. This rarely makes sense for minors who do not need a tax break today.\n\n---\n\n### Why Open a Custodial IRA?\n\n* **The Power of Compound Interest:** Because children have decades before they reach retirement age, even small contributions made during their teenage years have an incredibly long runway to compound and grow into a massive nest egg.\n* **Roth IRA Flexibility:** With a Roth IRA, the original contribution amounts can be withdrawn at any time, for any reason, without taxes or penalties. Additionally, there are exemptions that allow penalty-free withdrawals of earnings for qualifying expenses like college tuition or up to $10,000 for a first-time home purchase. \n* **Financial Literacy:** It is an excellent hands-on tool to teach teenagers about saving, compounding, the stock market, and long-term financial planning.\n\n---\n\n### What Happens When the Child Reaches Adulthood?\nWhen the child reaches the age of majority in their state (typically 18 or 21), the custodian must contact the financial institution to transition the account. The account is then converted into a standard, regular IRA under the child's sole control. At that point, the child can manage their own investments and continue contributing to the account on their own.",
